

# Initiative: Standardized Recruitment Email Communication Framework

## Description of Initiative / Procedure

This initiative focuses on designing and evaluating a standardized recruitment communication procedure for HAAG. Currently, recruitment emails (offers, rejections, waitlists, and reminders) are written independently by recruitment leads, resulting in inconsistencies in tone, clarity, and structure.

To address this, I am developing a reusable template system alongside a documented procedure that ensures all recruitment communication:

- Clearly communicates applicant status  
- Includes explicit deadlines and required actions  
- Maintains a consistent and professional tone  
- Reduces administrative workload  

The final output of this initiative will be:

- A template bank (offer, rejection, waitlist, and reminder emails)  
- A documented procedure for consistent implementation across future recruitment cycles  


## Hypotheses & KPIs

### Hypothesis

If standardized recruitment email templates are used, then communication will be clearer, more consistent, and easier for applicants to understand.

---

### KPIs Measured

At this stage, KPIs are evaluated using cognitive walkthroughs:

- Clarity of the decision (offer, rejection, waitlist)  
- Clarity of required actions  
- Presence and clarity of deadlines  
- Clarity of next steps  
- Consistency across templates  

---

### KPIs Not Yet Measured

Because there is no active recruitment cycle, the following have not been measured:

- Applicant response time  
- Number of clarification questions from applicants  
- Time saved in drafting emails  
- Stakeholder satisfaction  

---

## Testing Method (Flowchart)

Since no live testing is possible, this initiative uses cognitive walkthroughs.

**Flow:**

Create Templates  
→ Simulate Applicant Reading Email  
→ Check for Clarity (decision, action, deadlines, next steps)  
→ Identify Confusion  
→ Revise Templates

## Stakeholder Engagement

Stakeholder engagement has generally aligned with expectations, as feedback confirmed that the templates are clear and easy to follow. Minor suggestions helped refine wording and improve consistency.Because there is no active recruitment cycle, stakeholders are not interacting with the templates in a real-world setting. As a result, engagement is based on review and feedback rather than actual use.

In the future, stakeholder engagement would include:
- Recruitment leads using the templates in practice  
- Collecting feedback from applicants  
- Measuring how clearly the emails communicate decisions and next steps  


## Documentation & Sustainability

At this stage, I have begun documenting the recruitment communication procedure and drafting the standardized email templates.

All documentation is will be hosted in a GitHub repository, including:
- The recruitment communication procedure  
- Draft templates for offer, rejection, waitlist, and reminder emails  

This documentation is intended to make the process reusable and easy to adopt for future recruitment cycles.

Additional documentation planned includes:
- Finalized and polished versions of all templates  
- A brief implementation guide for recruitment leads  
- Notes on best practices for using and adapting the templates  

These steps will help ensure the initiative is sustainable and can be consistently applied in the future.

## Progress & Indicators

Progress toward this initiative has been measured through:
- Development of initial email templates  
- Evaluation through cognitive walkthroughs  
- Iteration and refinement of templates  

### Indicators of Success
- Templates clearly communicate decisions, actions, and deadlines  
- Improved consistency across all templates  
- Reduced ambiguity in wording  

### Indicators of Challenges

- Lack of real-world testing due to no active recruitment cycle  
- Limited ability to measure behavioral outcomes (e.g., applicant responses)  

## Obstacles & Bottlenecks

One of the main challenges in this initiative is the lack of an active recruitment cycle. This prevents real-world testing and limits evaluation to simulated methods such as cognitive walkthroughs.

An anticipated challenge that materialized was the inability to collect behavioral data, such as applicant responses or stakeholder feedback during actual use.

Additionally, it has been difficult to fully validate the effectiveness of the templates without real users interacting with them.

An unexpected challenge was balancing standardization with flexibility, ensuring the templates are consistent while still adaptable to different recruitment contexts.

